the primary purpose of node affinity feature is to ensure that pods rae hosted on particular nodes

like large data processing pod ends up larger node

the node affinity feature provides us with advanced capabilties to limit pod placement to particular nodes

#node affinity in pod definition file

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: myapp-pod
spec:
  containers:
    - name: my-app
      image: data-processor
  affinity:
    nodeAffinity:
      requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution
      nodeSelectorTerms:
        - matchExpressions:
           - key: size
              operator: In #esnures any values in the list of values provided below
              values:
                - Large
                - Medium
          

what if there are nodes with label app=web

#irrespective of any scheduling type(once the pod is scheduled, kubernetes will run that pod on that node)
#lets say we our pod is scheduled on the node with label app=web
#what is someone changes the label in furture, while the pods that matches the labels(tolerant) are running on that node
#will the pod continue to stay on the node?
--all of this is answered by a property under nodeAffinity, which is type of nodeAffinity

nodeAffinity types: they define the behaviour of the scheduler w.r.t nodeAffinity and also in stages in the life cycle of pod

2 types of nodeAffinity

requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution
preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution

additoional type of nodeAffinity
requiredDuringSchedulingRequiredDuringExecution

there are two stage in the lifecycle of the pod when considering nodeAffinity

a) during scheduling: is the stage where pod does not exist and is created for the first time
       #which is affinity rules are considered to place the pod on the right nodes
       #what if the nodes with matching labels are not available?, we forgot to lable the node as large
       #this is where type of node affinity comes into play
    
    1) requiredDuringScheduling: scheduler will mandar that pod to be placed on node with given affinity rules in pod definition file, if it cannot find one, the pod will not be scheduled

    this type is used, where placement of pod in particular node is crucial

    2) preferredDuringScheduling:
    lets say pod placement on particular node is less important, than running the workload itself
    in that case you could set it to preferred 
    if in case a matching node is not found, the scheduler will ignore the affinity rules and and place the pod on any available node
    it tries to place the pod in best matching node among the affinity rule

b) during execution 
 this is stage of pod, where pod is running on a node and a change is made in the environment that affects node affinity rules in the pod, such as change in the lable of node 

#remember node labels can be removed/added/updated at any time
 ex: an adminster removed the label on node size=large, what would happen to the pod that are running on node?

if 1)"IgnoredDuringExecution" : pods will continue to run, even if there are any changes in the node labels

#remember you cannot change the labels, node affinity rules in the pod, those are immutable
#even if you change them,in pod definition file, you need to delete the current pod and create new one
(kubectl apply -f definition.yaml(it will delete exisiting one and create new one, if pods are part of replicaset then current pods stay same, new pods getting created will have the updated affinity, labels etc))

  2) RequiredDuringExecution: if there any changes in the labels on node and if that change is affecting the affinty rules of already running pods, then
    those pods affecting will be evicted from node

    """who handles the recreation of such pod?""", need to check

    No, requiredDuringSchedulingRequiredDuringExecution is not implemented in Kubernetes. It was originally proposed as a planned feature to evict running Pods if a node's labels changed later, but it was never built into the Kubernetes API.




